Defect: with OSSL_WINCTX, OpenSSL on Windows reads OPENSSLDIR from the registry for the configuration file and the default certificates, but CTLOG_STORE_load_default_file still opens the CT log list in the OPENSSLDIR of the build (CTLOG_FILE), a folder that any Windows user can create, such as C:\workdir\...\ssl. A user who plants ct_log_list.cnf there chooses the CT logs that programs trust.
Upstream status: not reported upstream yet; this patch takes the CT log list from the OPENSSLDIR of the registry key, as the configuration file.
Version-bump check: build the Windows targets and confirm that the build check of tasks/openssl/build finds no build folder in libcrypto.a; drop this patch when ct_log.c uses ossl_get_openssldir() under OSSL_WINCTX.

--- a/crypto/ct/ct_log.c
+++ b/crypto/ct/ct_log.c
@@ -161,9 +161,26 @@
 int CTLOG_STORE_load_default_file(CTLOG_STORE *store)
 {
     const char *fpath = ossl_safe_getenv(CTLOG_FILE_EVP);
+#if defined(_WIN32) && defined(OSSL_WINCTX)
+    char default_path[1024];
+#endif
 
-    if (fpath == NULL)
+    if (fpath == NULL) {
+#if defined(_WIN32) && defined(OSSL_WINCTX)
+        /*
+         * The CT log list lies in the OPENSSLDIR of the registry key, like the
+         * configuration file, not in the OPENSSLDIR of the build.
+         */
+        const char *dir = ossl_get_openssldir();
+
+        if (dir == NULL || dir[0] == '\0')
+            return 0;
+        BIO_snprintf(default_path, sizeof(default_path), "%s/ct_log_list.cnf", dir);
+        fpath = default_path;
+#else
         fpath = CTLOG_FILE;
+#endif
+    }
 
     return CTLOG_STORE_load_file(store, fpath);
 }
